mber(@dateA) AS A ON P.ProjectID = A.ProjectID\nLEFT JOIN dbo.fn_ProjectNumber(@dateB) AS B ON P.ProjectID = B.ProjectID --<<<<<"""\n\nCase 3:\nStep 1: The original query is using different methods to search for a string that meets a condition. This can be rewritten as a single method.\nQuery Rewrite 1: """There would be no reason to use a NOT LIKE. Whether you use the != or the <> form of the inequality operator is solely a matter of personal preference."""\n\nCase 4:\nStep 1: The original query is using two separate queries to get data from two tables. This can be rewritten as a single JOINed query.\nQuery Rewrite 1: """The first one. It\'s only a single round trip. It requires a little processing to collapse the rows of authors into a comma-separated list like you want but that\'s basically boilerplate code.\n\nSeparate related queries are a bad habit that will kill your performance faster than most things."""\n\nCase 5:\nStep 1: The original query is using a simple query with a prepared statement. This can be rewritten as a more efficient and secure method.\nQuery Rewrite 1: """Performance wise you can create an index on `id` if not already done.\n\nSecurity wise you are open to SQL-injection attack, use prepares statements instead."""\n\nNote that these query rewrite approaches may or may not improve the performance of the original queries. The effectiveness of each approach depends on various factors such as the size and complexity of the data, the indexing and schema design of the tables, and the specific requirements of the application.', 'time': 6.6988444328308105}
