    and ws3.d_year =2002\n    and case when ws1.web_sales > 0 then ws2.web_sales/ws1.web_sales else null end\n       > case when ss1.store_sales > 0 then ss2.store_sales/ss1.store_sales else null end\n    and case when ws2.web_sales > 0 then ws3.web_sales/ws2.web_sales else null end\n       > case when ss2.store_sales > 0 then ss3.store_sales/ss2.store_sales else null end\n order by web_q1_q2_increase;\n```\n\nQuery Rewrite Strategies:\nQuery Rewrite 1:\n"""The rewritten query has the simplified expressions in the SELECT list. For example, the `web_q1_q2_increase` and `store_q1_q2_increase` columns are calculated as `/($19(ws_ext_sales_price), $15(ws_ext_sales_price))` and `/($7(ss_ext_sales_price), $3(ss_ext_sales_price))`, respectively. These expressions can be evaluated to a constant, which is the result of the simplification.\n\nThe query rewrite rule applies Case 1 of the rule, which simplifies constant arithmetic expressions or nested functions that can be evaluated to a constant. The rewritten query has simplified expressions in the SELECT list, which can be evaluated to a constant."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""Since the original query matches Case 1 of the query rewrite rule, the rewritten query applies the aggregate functions and `GROUP BY` clause to the data source as shown in the logical plan changes after rewrite.\n\nThe rewritten query is a more efficient and optimized version of the original query."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""Try this... I added an inner join and added a username to your select clause. The MIN() is just a way to include the username column in the select and will not have an impact on your result as long as you have just one username for every Killerid.\n\nSELECT `killerID`, COUNT(`killerID`) AS tot_kills, MIN(`Username`) AS username\nFROM `kills`\nINNER JOIN `players` ON `players`.`id` = `kills`.`killerid`\nWHERE `killText` LIKE \'%$gun%\'\nGROUP BY `killerID`\nORDER BY `tot_kills` DESC"""\n\nQuery Rewrite 4:\n"""Try fixing the join, you refer to the wrong alias in the second LEFT JOIN:\n\nSELECT P.ProjectName, A.Number, B.Number\nFROM Project AS P\nLEFT JOIN dbo.fn_ProjectNumber(@dateA) AS A ON P.ProjectID = A.ProjectID\nLEFT JOIN dbo.fn_ProjectNumber(@dateB) AS B ON P.ProjectID = B.ProjectID --<<<<<"""\n\nQuery Rewrite 5:\n"""There would be no reason to use a NOT LIKE. Whether you use the != or the <> form of the inequality operator is solely a matter of personal preference."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 6:\n"""The first one. It\'s only a single round trip. It requires a little processing to collapse the rows of authors into a comma-separated list like you want but that\'s basically boilerplate code.\n\nSeparate related queries are a bad habit that will kill your performance faster than most things."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 7:\n"""Performance wise you can create an index on `id` if not already done.\n\nSecurity wise you are open to SQL-injection attack, use prepares statements instead."""'}], 'response': "Here is a summary of the provided query rewrite strategies in one paragraph:\n\nThe query rewrites aim to simplify and optimize the given SQL query. Query Rewrite 1 simplifies constant arithmetic expressions or nested functions that can be evaluated to a constant. Query Rewrite 2 applies aggregate functions and GROUP BY clause to the data source, resulting in a more efficient query. Query Rewrite 3 suggests adding an inner join and including a username column in the select statement. Query Rewrite 4 fixes a join issue by referring to the correct alias. Query Rewrite 5 notes that there is no reason to use NOT LIKE, as it's equivalent to != or <>. Query Rewrite 6 recommends collapsing rows of authors into a comma-separated list for better performance. Finally, Query Rewrite 7 suggests creating an index on `id` for performance and using prepared statements to prevent SQL injection attacks.", 'time': 1.8231604099273682}
